      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "11"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2011",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues *Expanding...* (<htcite0>). We therefore suggest studying that text first, before moving on to this one.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6f9bd52-c0cb-497e-9217-1f9677d5b491",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Expanding...",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we focus too much on a single detail, we risk overlooking everything around it. To see why, let us look again at the example of the sheet divided into two halves, separated in the center:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Divided Sheet</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td><td style=\"height: 300px;\"></td></tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Sheet divided into two parts, separated in the center.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In Table 1, we see that the two sides are separated by a central line. Let us carry out one more exercise with these sheets:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_lab_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_lab question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-microscope\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_lab\"><b>Practice 1: The Third Part</b><br /><br />1. If you don’t have the sheet you previously divided, take a new one and cut it in two.<br />2. Bring the two parts as close together as possible. Notice that, even so, there is still something separating them in the center.<br />3. Now, hold one part in each hand and move them as far apart as you can. At this stage, it becomes clear that there is much more than just two sides — which have now become extremes. Notice that the center you saw before did not disappear: it is now one more region between the two extremes.</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "This practice, together with the ideas from the previous texts, leads us to an initial reflection:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: On Extremes</b><br /><br />When we look only at ourselves and at an opposing side, could this limited view lead us to extremes?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_body",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Human Body\n\nTo better understand how the division into three appears in daily life, let us begin with the human body, shown in **Figure 1**:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img  id=\"imgHHB\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgHHB', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Human Body. © History Tracers. <span id=\"htImgCopyright\"></span>.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "For didactic purposes, let us imagine the human body divided into three parts:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen tableTenBgBlue\"><tr><th>Body part</th><th>Description</th></tr> <tr><td>Head (1)</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">The upper region of the body, where the eyes, nose, and mouth are located.</td></tr> <tr><td>Trunk</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">Includes the neck (2), chest (3), abdomen (4), and pelvis (5).</td></tr> <tr><td>Limbs</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">Includes the arms (6), hands (7), legs (8), and feet (9).</td></tr> </table> </p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Description of the regions of the human body.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Even though we start from three parts, we use nine numbers to represent the internal regions inside those divisions. In other words, the number of groups we choose depends on how much detail we need. As we move forward in our study, we will look at each part of the human body in greater detail.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_indigenous",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The Number Three among Indigenous Peoples\n\nIn the previous text, we saw, for example, the understanding of the universe divided into underworld, earth, and heavens in the Mesoamerican ballgame (<htcite0>). However, Indigenous peoples used the number three in many other contexts. Let us look at two of them: the preparation of food and the measurement of the seasons.\n\n#### Cuisine\n\nWhen we analyze the tools used to prepare food — such as the metate from Teotihuacan, Mexico — we see that they rested on a tripod, that is, a support with three legs:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6f9bd52-c0cb-497e-9217-1f9677d5b491",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Expanding...",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"metate3\"  class=\"imgcenter\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('metate3', '0%')\" /><b>Figure 2</b>: Metate from Mexico. Photo taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the Archaeological Site of Teotihuacan, México (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0059a36e-b134-4859-bec0-c89e7239d07c",
                     "text": "INAH. Teotihuacan",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "10",
                     "day": "19"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "In this metate, we see two supports at the front (“legs”) and a third support at the back, at the center of a curved rectangular slab. On top of it rests a roller used to grind corn. Count them: three supports in total.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Also in Mesoamerica (*Abya Yala*), following the classification proposed by W. Creamer (<htcite0>), we find ceramic vessels resting on three supports, such as those discovered in Costa Rica:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "2d720f02-051c-4469-b425-7d020f2c2f9b",
                     "text": "Creamer, W.",
                     "page": "pp. 35 - 37",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1987",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"cr0\"  class=\"imgcenter\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('cr0', '0%')\" /><b>Figure 3</b>: Ceramic vessels. Photograph taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the Jade Museum in Costa Rica (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a04504ce-6eb1-41b0-8045-c461c82dc8b5",
                     "text": "Museo del Jade",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "2",
                     "day": "6"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "In this figure, we see several ceramic vessels resting on three supports. They come in different shapes, such as trays and jars.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### E-Group Complexes\n\nThe E-Group complexes, found in different regions of Mesoamerica (*Abya Yala*), are still not fully understood (<htcite0>). Some studies, however, suggest that they may have been used to measure the seasons of the year (<htcite1>). If this is confirmed, we would have an interesting case: the four seasons of the year represented by a structure with only three elevated constructions. Those three constructions, in turn, would have marked the solstices — the days with the longest or shortest nights, depending on the Earth’s position — and the equinoxes, when day and night have the same length (<htcite2>). Three constructions, but four seasons: once again, the number three opens a wider range than the number two.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "8ecdaf57-d55d-4d95-bad5-f76e0f671483",
                     "text": "Šprajc, I.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2021",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "e7c7458b-0c63-4485-9c7b-4640edcd9122",
                     "text": "Inomata, T.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2017",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "dfab6e33-fe94-4aec-8d9b-ed02ca8402c8",
                     "text": "Martz de la Vega, H.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2021",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/UBMUR-3hHz0?si=mn22_mrWLJ23ueeO\" title=\"YouTube video player\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen style=\"aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; width: 55% !important;\"></iframe><br />Video 1: Type E Complex in Caracol, Belize. Recorded during a visit to the site on <htdate0>.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "4",
                     "day": "26"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_archaeology",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Excavating\n\nWhen we already have an idea of what a culture or an object is like, analyzing only a part of it may allow us to imagine the whole. This happens in different areas of science. In this text, we will focus on archaeology.\n\nBefore continuing, let us reflect for a moment:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Eureka</b><br /><br />When we find parts of something, is it possible to reconstruct it?<br /><br />Is it possible to have a mistaken idea when observing only a part?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Inside an excavation\n\nWhen an excavation begins at an archaeological site, we cannot know in advance every object that will be found. Still, if we know the cultures related to the site, we can form a general idea of what might appear.\n\n**Figure 4** shows the excavation of a sacred mountain (*Witz*) in San Andrés, El Salvador (<htcite0>; <htcite1>). Notice that the structure was completely covered with earth over time.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "960e3a8e-3a60-4ee4-9df4-e2187489e3b9",
                     "text": "García Capistrán, H.",
                     "page": "pp. 144",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4a518e58-fedf-4620-a7d6-2fb2f1fd5033",
                     "text": "Parque Arqueológico San Andrés",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img  id=\"imgSCP\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgSCP', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 4</b>: Photo taken at the San Andrés Archaeological Park on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4a518e58-fedf-4620-a7d6-2fb2f1fd5033",
                     "text": "Parque Arqueológico San Andrés",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2015",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "25"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Excavating a structure is a slow and careful process, intended, among other things, to avoid breaking the pieces. **Figure 5** shows an excavation at Cahal Pech, Belize (<htcite0>), together with the tools used in the work. As we can see, they are simple hand tools: a bucket, a shovel, and a hammer.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "bdc3e24e-2029-4a4b-8fc3-116feed24586",
                     "text": "National Institute of Culture and History",
                     "page": "Cahal Pech",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img  id=\"imgCPE\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgCPE', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 5</b>: Photo taken at the archaeological site of Cahal Pech, Belize, on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "bdc3e24e-2029-4a4b-8fc3-116feed24586",
                     "text": "Cahal Pech",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "10",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "19"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_math",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Reconstructing the excavation\n\nDespite all the knowledge and techniques used to carry out excavations and preserve artifacts, it is still common to find objects broken or deformed by time. In this case, we rely on what we learned at other sites and in earlier studies to reconstruct these pieces.\n\n#### Study scenario\n\nSince Belize is also part of Mesoamerica (*Abya Yala*), we can use what we already know about pieces from the region to begin reconstructing those found broken during excavations.\n\nIn a real scenario, we would first use data from the same site and then from nearby sites. Since this is an educational text, we have the freedom to go a little further and explore broader connections.\n\n#### Mapping\n\nIn Teotihuacan, to the north, and in Costa Rica, to the south, pieces with three supports have been found. Let us suppose, then, that we have pieces like those in **Figure 3**, all broken, and that we need to put them back together.\n\nWhen we separate the pieces into groups, we count a total of 27 supports (“legs”) among the objects.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: Reconstructing</b><br /><br />We know that each piece uses 3 supports. With 27 supports available, how many complete pieces can we assemble? Take a moment to work it out before reading on.</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Calculating\n\nLet us begin by writing what we know so far as a mathematical formula:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p class=\"htFormulaText\"><b>Total supports</b> = <b>Total pieces</b> × <b>number of supports</b></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: The total number of supports equals the number of pieces times the number of supports in each piece.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now we can substitute the values: *Total supports* = 27 and *number of supports* per piece = 3, the same number observed in different places. This gives us the number of pieces we are looking for:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>27</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mi>Total pieces</mi><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mi>3</mi></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: 27 supports organized into pieces with 3 supports each.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "To find the result, we need to solve **Calculation 1**. For this, we can use what we have learned in this chapter together with the multiplication table of 3, the same one presented in Chapter 4 of *First Steps* (<htcite0>). Let us look at the table:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "72dcaa0f-07e3-41de-a637-c5b2f528dc33",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (2 and 3)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent3\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "On the left we have the products of the table of 3, each one 3 greater than the previous one. There we can find 27 = 3 × 9, which tells us that 27 supports grouped into sets of 3 fill 9 sets, so *Total pieces* = 9. We can confirm this result by undoing **Calculation 1**: we divide both sides of the equality by 3:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
